      "goal_md": "> **Note (Stage 01 — unverified draft):** Benchmark names and SOTA claims in this file are LLM-generated estimates. They will be verified during the literature search stage.\n\n# Topic: Stale or Malicious? Disconnection-Induced Failures of History-Based Byzantine Defences in Asynchronous Federated Learning\n\n## Novel Angle\n\n### The Unexplored Gap\nWhile Byzantine-robust Federated Learning (FL) and Asynchronous Federated Learning (AFL) are both heavily researched, their intersection contains a critical, unaddressed vulnerability: **the confounding effect of network-induced staleness on history-based anomaly detection.** \n\nModern Byzantine defenses (such as Sageflow, Kardam, or historical gradient similarity tracking) rely on the consistency of a client's update trajectory (their \"history\") to flag malicious actors. However, in realistic edge networks, benign clients frequently experience temporary disconnections, deep sleep cycles, or transient network jitter. When these \"stragglers\" reconnect, they submit updates computed on highly stale global parameters. \n\nThis creates two severe, unexplored failure modes:\n1. **False Positives (Denial of Service to Benign Clients):** Because a reconnected client's update is highly stale, its gradient direction deviates significantly from the current active consensus trajectory. History-based defenses misclassify these stale, benign updates as malicious, permanently penalizing or excluding honest edge devices and causing representation collapse.\n2. **Exploitation (Staleness Masking / Mimicry Attacks):** A Byzantine attacker can intentionally delay their poisoned updates to mimic the staleness profile of a reconnected straggler. By hiding behind the \"staleness tolerance\" thresholds of history-based defenses, attackers can inject backdoors or poison the model undetected.\n\n### Why This is Timely and Different\nExisting AFL works treat staleness purely as an optimization challenge (solved via learning rate scaling), while robust FL works assume synchronous rounds or smooth, continuous client participation. No existing work systematically addresses how bursty, long-term client disconnections break the mathematical assumptions of history-based Byzantine filters. Proposing a lightweight, history-agnostic alignment method to decouple staleness from malice represents a highly novel, high-impact contribution.\n\n---\n\n## Trend Validation\n\n### Research Trend & Motivation\nIn decentralized and edge-deployed ML, asynchronous updates are replacing synchronous rounds to maximize hardware utilization. Concurrently, security research is shifting from simple spatial outlier detection (e.g., coordinate-wise median, Krum) to temporal/historical analysis because spatial defenses are easily bypassed by adaptive, low-magnitude attacks. This project bridges this gap by exposing how temporal defenses fail when the physical network layer introduces temporal anomalies (staleness).\n\n### Benchmark\n* **Name:** **FEMNIST** (Federated Extended MNIST from the LEAF benchmark) and **CIFAR-10** (partitioned using a Dirichlet distribution $\\alpha = 0.2$ to simulate realistic non-IID client data).\n* **Source:** LEAF Benchmark Suite / Standard PyTorch Vision Datasets.\n* **Metrics:** \n  * *Global Model Test Accuracy* (under attack).\n  * *Attack Success Rate (ASR)* (for backdoor/targeted poisoning).\n  * *False Positive Rate (FPR)* (percentage of benign, stale updates incorrectly rejected by the defense).\n  * *Defensive Latency* (compute overhead per update).\n* **SOTA Tracking:** Performance under Byzantine attacks on non-IID data is actively tracked on platforms like Papers with Code under the \"Robust Federated Learning\" and \"Asynchronous Federated Learning\" tasks.\n\n---\n\n## Scope\n* **In-Scope:**\n  * Simulating an AFL environment with heterogeneous client connection profiles (specifically modeling bursty, long-term disconnections).\n  * Evaluating three SOTA history-based defenses: Kardam, Sageflow, and a baseline Historical Cosine Similarity filter.\n  * Implementing two attack vectors: a standard Inner Product Manipulation (IPM) attack and a novel \"Staleness-Masked\" adaptive attack.\n  * Designing a lightweight mitigation strategy (e.g., gradient projection onto the current active subspace to \"de-stale\" updates before anomaly detection).\n* **Out-of-Scope:**\n  * Sybil attacks or secure multi-party computation (SMPC) cryptographic primitives.\n  * Large Language Model (LLM) scale federated tuning (constrained by compute).\n\n---\n\n## SMART Goal\n\n* **Specific:** Quantify the failure rates of three history-based Byzantine defenses under bursty client disconnections in AFL, demonstrate a novel \"staleness-masked\" attack vector, and develop a projection-based alignment mitigation.\n* **Measurable:** \n  * Demonstrate that bursty disconnections cause standard history-based defenses to suffer a False Positive Rate (FPR) of $>30\\%$ on benign clients.\n  * Show that the proposed \"Staleness-Masked\" attack achieves $>50\\%$ Attack Success Rate (ASR) against these defenses.\n  * Achieve an FPR of $<5\\%$ and an ASR of $<10\\%$ using our proposed mitigation, while maintaining global model accuracy within $2\\%$ of an attack-free baseline.\n* **Achievable:** The simulation framework can be built on top of PyTorch and Ray. Running a complete training run of 100 clients on CIFAR-10/FEMNIST for 200 virtual epochs takes approximately 3–4 hours on a single modern GPU.\n* **Relevant:** Directly addresses critical security and reliability vulnerabilities in edge-deployed ML systems (e.g., mobile devices, autonomous vehicles, IoT).\n* **Time-bound:** Completed within 8 weeks:\n  * *Weeks 1-2:* Implement AFL simulation framework with bursty disconnection profiles.\n  * *Weeks 3-4:* Benchmark existing defenses and demonstrate the \"Staleness-Masked\" attack.\n  * *Weeks 5-6:* Develop, integrate, and refine the projection-based mitigation.\n  * *Weeks 7-8:* Collect final experimental data and draft the manuscript.\n\n---\n\n## Constraints\n\n* **Compute Budget:** A single local workstation with 1x NVIDIA RTX 4090 (24GB VRAM) and 64GB System RAM.\n* **Available Tools:** PyTorch, Ray (for parallelizing client updates), NumPy, Scikit-learn.\n* **Data Access:** Open-source, standard datasets (CIFAR-10, FEMNIST via LEAF) requiring no special licensing or data-sharing agreements.\n\n---\n\n## Success Criteria\n\n1. **Vulnerability Demonstration:** Clear empirical proof that realistic client disconnections cause a severe drop in global model accuracy due to the false rejection of benign stale updates.\n2. **Attack Efficacy:** Successful demonstration that an attacker can exploit the staleness-tolerance window to bypass history-based filters, raising the Attack Success Rate (ASR) by at least $3\\times$ compared to non-masked attacks.\n3. **Mitigation Performance:** The proposed mitigation successfully distinguishes between stale-benign and fresh/stale-malicious updates, outperforming standard history-based defenses by at least $15\\%$ in final global model accuracy under joint network churn and Byzantine attacks.\n4. **Publishability:** The findings, formalization of the vulnerability, and proposed solution are compiled into a paper suitable for submission to top-tier ML/Security venues (e.g., NeurIPS, ICLR, or USENIX Security).\n\n---\n\n## Generated\n2023-10-27 14:30:00 (UTC)"
